[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this differently. The 6×6 case has 4 forced corner hooks, and[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthen the remaining 12 cells can't be covered by 2 hooks. This is a structural [0m
[38;2;124;124;124margument specific to the 6×6 geometry.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a general m×n with m,n ≡ 2 mod 4, the 4 corners force 4 hooks. After placing[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthese, the remaining cells form a specific pattern. The question is whether this[0m[38;2;124;124;124mpattern can be tiled.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut this approach is hard to generalize because the remaining pattern depends on[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe orientations of the corner hooks.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me try a different approach. Let me think about the problem in terms of a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m"transfer matrix" or "boundary condition" argument.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me reconsider. Maybe the condition isn't exactly "4|m or 4|n" but [0m[38;2;124;124;124msomething else that happens to coincide with my computational results. Let me [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcheck a few more cases.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me check m=2, n=6: 12|12, but m=2 < 3. NO (trivially).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mm=2, n=12: 12|24, m=2 < 3. NO.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mm=6, n=6: NO. ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mm=10, n=10: 12|100? No. NO (trivially, 12∤100).[0m
