[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me try a different approach. Let me consider the "edge" cells. In [0m[38;2;124;124;124man m×n rectangle, the cells on the boundary (row 0, row m-1, col 0, col n-1) can[0m[38;2;124;124;124monly be covered by hooks that extend to the boundary. Specifically:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Row 0 cells can only be covered by hooks at row 0.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Row m-1 cells can only be covered by hooks at row m-3.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Col 0 cells can only be covered by hooks at col 0.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Col n-1 cells can only be covered by hooks at col n-3.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis gives structural constraints on the tiling.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the top edge (row 0): hooks at row 0 cover 3 consecutive columns in row 0. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThe number of cells covered in row 0 by a hook at (0,c) is the row-0 count of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe hook, which is 1, 2, or 3. The total cells in row 0 is n, and they must all [0m[38;2;124;124;124mbe covered by hooks at row 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe hooks at row 0 span columns c to c+2. They can start at c = 0, 1, ..., n-3. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mThe hooks at row 0 must cover all n cells in row 0, with each cell covered [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexactly once.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a 1-dimensional tiling problem: tile a 1×n strip with "pieces" of length[0m[38;2;124;124;124m3 (covering 3 consecutive positions), where each piece covers 1, 2, or 3 of the [0m[38;2;124;124;124m3 positions (the rest are covered by hooks at other rows). But the pieces can [0m
[38;2;124;124;124moverlap in their column ranges (they just can't overlap in the cells they cover [0m[38;2;124;124;124min row 0).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, this is more subtle. A hook at (0,c) covers some cells in row 0 [0m
